8,8,9,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids contain.** Each grid has one background colour and straight lines of one colour (6 in pair 1, 3 in pair 2, 9 in the test). Some lines have gaps. At some gaps the line seems to have \"slipped\": a short diagonal staircase of single cells comes off the line end. There are also loose diagonal pieces that touch no line.\n\n**What the output does (from the changed-cell list).**\n- *Pair 1:*\n  - The staircase (8,17),(9,16) leaves the end of the row-7 segment at (7,18). In the output it is erased, and row 7 is filled straight left over (7,15)…(7,17) until it meets the 6 at (7,14).\n  - The staircase (10,13),(11,12) leaves the bottom of the column-14 line at (9,14). It is erased, and column 14 is filled down over (10..12,14) to meet the segment starting at (13,14).\n  - The loose diagonal (3,19),(4,20),(5,21) touches no line, so it is simply erased.\n- *Pair 2:* the same thing happens 3 times: vertical gaps at x=18 and x=11 are filled, and the gap in row 9 to the right of x=19 is filled. Every diagonal piece is erased.\n- *Gaps without a staircase stay open.* Examples are row 9, x=7..9 and the gaps in column 4 of pair 2.\n\n**Rule.**\n1. Find the \"pure diagonal\" chains. These are cells joined by a diagonal step whose two shared orthogonal neighbours are both background.\n2. In each chain, the **root** is the cell with exactly one orthogonal foreground neighbour. That is the line end the staircase hangs from.\n3. Erase every chain cell except its root.\n4. From the root, continue straight away from its orthogonal neighbour. If this ray reaches another line cell, fill the gap with the line colour. If it runs off the grid, the root was only a stray stub, so erase it as well.\n\nThe reference program implements exactly this rule and reproduces the changed cells of both pairs. I reuse it with small cleanups.\n\n**Test input.**\n- The staircase (4,4),(5,3) hangs from (3,5), whose only orthogonal neighbour is (3,6). The ray goes left and fills (3,2..4) up to (3,1).\n- The staircase (20,15),(21,16) hangs from (19,14). The ray goes down and fills (20..22,14) up to (23,14).\n- The piece (15,2),(16,3) hangs below row 14. Its ray runs down and off the grid, so both cells are erased.\n- The loose pair (11,12),(12,11) is erased.\n- The plain gaps (row 14 x=10..12, and (26,14)) stay as they are.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    fg = g != bg\n    ys, xs = np.nonzero(fg)\n    if len(ys) == 0:\n        emit(g.copy(), \"Nothing to repair.\")\n        return g\n    col = int(g[ys[0], xs[0]])\n\n    def isfg(y, x):\n        return 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and fg[y, x]\n\n    # diagonal steps whose two orthogonal 'corner' cells are both background\n    cells = list(zip(*np.nonzero(fg)))\n    adj = {c: [] for c in cells}\n    for (y, x) in cells:\n        for dy in (-1, 1):\n            for dx in (-1, 1):\n                ny, nx = y + dy, x + dx\n                if isfg(ny, nx) and not isfg(y, nx) and not isfg(ny, x):\n                    adj[(y, x)].append((ny, nx))\n    seen = set()\n    groups = []\n    for c in cells:\n        if c in seen or not adj[c]:\n            continue\n        stack = [c]; seen.add(c); comp = []\n        while stack:\n            p = stack.pop(); comp.append(p)\n            for q in adj[p]:\n                if q not in seen:\n                    seen.add(q); stack.append(q)\n        if len(comp) >= 2:\n            groups.append(comp)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    roots = []\n    for comp in groups:\n        root = None\n        for (y, x) in comp:\n            orth = [(y + dy, x + dx) for dy, dx in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))\n                    if isfg(y + dy, x + dx)]\n            if len(orth) == 1:\n                root = ((y, x), orth[0])\n                break\n        for c in comp:\n            if root is None or c != root[0]:\n                out[c] = bg\n        if root is not None:\n            roots.append(root)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Erase the diagonal staircases, keeping only the line cell each one hangs from.\")\n\n    for (ry, rx), (oy, ox) in roots:\n        dy, dx = ry - oy, rx - ox\n        path = []\n        y, x = ry + dy, rx + dx\n        hit = False\n        while 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n            if out[y, x] != bg:\n                hit = True\n                break\n            path.append((y, x))\n            y += dy; x += dx\n        if not hit:\n            out[ry, rx] = bg\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Erase the stub at ({ry},{rx}): its straight extension reaches no line.\")\n        elif path:\n            for p in path:\n                out[p] = col\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Extend the line straight from ({ry},{rx}) across the gap of {len(path)} cells.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rays":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"If this ray reaches another line cell, fill the gap with the line colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The ray goes left and fills (3,2..4) up to (3,1)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The ray goes down and fills (20..22,14) up to (23,14)."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":true,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"75b71d14aef054219a025a96d834b90c3b07dbbd8cf395002ae0ca13c695d13b","task_sha256":"44be0c63d1a93ee2058f305462c3c0cee9c6c329ef416bbb590c235c6a776fe4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
